<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hattians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hattian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hattians were the earlier inhabitants of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, speaking a non-Indo-European language, before the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. The Hittites adopted the land's name, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a>, and absorbed many Hattian religious traditions and deities. They were thus an important foundation of Hittite culture.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Hattians inhabited the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. They spoke the Hattian language, which was neither Semitic nor Indo-European. They are attested archaeologically from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> and in later Hittite and other sources, and their main centre was the city of Hattush. Scholars use the names as a convention for the pre-Hittite population of central Anatolia, and it is not known whether related inhabitants of neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> ever saw themselves as Hattians. The term Proto-Hittite is inaccurate, because Hittite is an Indo-European language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological sites in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> from the second half of the 3rd millennium BCE are attributed to the Hattians. At Hattush the finds show a complex culture with social stratification. Most scholars believe the first Hattian states existed in the period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>, on the basis of later Hittite and Assyrian sources. A Hittite version of a story about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, from about 1400 BCE, names Pamba of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a> among 17 allied kings, although this is not attested in Akkadian versions or contemporary sources.</p><p>The Hattians were organised in monarchical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, ruled as theocratic kingdoms or principalities. Assyrian merchants set up trading colonies called karum throughout eastern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and during the first centuries of the 2nd millennium BCE an Assyrian colony existed in Hattush. Faced with Hittite expansion from about 2000 BCE, the Hattians were gradually absorbed, by about 1700 BCE, into the new order. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> kept the name of the land, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a>, and made it the usual name of their own state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>About 150 short specimens of Hattian text have been found among Hittite cuneiform tablets, mostly religious or cultic in character, and Hattian appears to have remained alive until at least the end of the 14th century BCE. Some scholars relate it to the Northwest Caucasian languages. Hattian religion involved worship of the earth, personified as a mother goddess, to ensure good harvests. The pantheon included the storm-god Taru, represented by a bull, and the sun-goddess Furušemu, represented by a leopard. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> took over much of it, and the legends of Telipinu and the dragon Illuyanka originate in Hattian civilisation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hattians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religious and literary traditions absorbed from the <b>Hattians</b> and Hurrians were transmitted westward, and the Hurro-Hittite Kumarbi cycle, with its succession of warring sky gods, closely parallels Hesiod's Theogony, one of the earliest documented channels of cultural transmission between the Near East and Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
